dux-mono-regular.woff2 and dux-mono-bold.woff2 are Roboto Mono Regular and
Bold (weights 400 and 700, instanced from the variable font), from
googlefonts/robotomono, tag v3.001
(https://github.com/googlefonts/robotomono), file
fonts/variable/RobotoMono[wght].ttf. Licensed OFL-1.1.

dux-mono-symbols.woff2 is a subset of JetBrains Mono Nerd Font Mono Regular,
from ryanoasis/nerd-fonts release v3.5.0
(https://github.com/ryanoasis/nerd-fonts), cut to the structural glyph
ranges dux needs to draw TUI-shaped output: arrows (U+2190-21FF),
miscellaneous technical (U+2300-23FF), box drawing and block elements
(U+2500-25FF), miscellaneous symbols and dingbats (U+2600-27BF), braille
patterns (U+2800-28FF), and the Powerline private-use glyphs
(U+E0A0-E0D7). Licensed OFL-1.1.

dux-mono-fill.woff2 is a subset of Adwaita Mono Regular, name-table version
34.1.0, taken from the Arch package adwaita-fonts 50.0-1
(/usr/share/fonts/Adwaita/AdwaitaMono-Regular.ttf). Upstream is
GNOME/adwaita-fonts (https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/adwaita-fonts), a build
of Iosevka. Note this is a weaker provenance claim than the two faces
above, which name exact upstream release tags; 34.1.0 is the font's own
internal version string, not an adwaita-fonts tag.

It is the backstop face: it carries the symbol-block glyphs Adwaita Mono
has that neither of the two faces above covers, so a device whose own
installed fonts lack a glyph still gets one. Four deliberate exclusions,
which together give the face one invariant worth stating plainly, that
every glyph in it occupies exactly one terminal cell:

  - Glyphs whose advance is not 600/1000 are dropped, so nothing lands off
    the cell grid. That is very nearly, but not exactly, the ratio the text
    face uses: Roboto Mono is 1229/2048 = 0.6000977 and this face is 0.6000
    even, a 0.016% difference, 0.0014 px at 14 px. It does not accumulate,
    because xterm measures each rendered character and compensates with
    per-span letter-spacing.
  - Code points with Unicode Emoji_Presentation=Yes are dropped: those are
    grid-width-2 and belong to the platform colour-emoji font.
  - Code points with East_Asian_Width W or F are dropped, for the same
    reason read the other way round: the terminal allots them two cells,
    this face would draw them one cell wide, and they would sit in the left
    half of their slot. Dropping them lets a font that can draw them at
    full width have them. This empties the Yijing hexagram block, which is
    therefore not in the declared unicode-range at all.
  - The whole Private Use Area is dropped. The PUA exists precisely so that
    other people's fonts can put things there, so a bundled backstop has no
    business claiming it: those slots belong to whatever Nerd Font the
    visitor has installed. Two of Iosevka's own PUA glyphs were ink-less,
    which would have rendered as nothing at all where a visitor's font had
    a real glyph.

Licensed OFL-1.1.

None of the three upstream OFL.txt files declares a Reserved Font Name
after its copyright statement, so OFL clause 3 does not, on its own,
require any of these files to be renamed. Every file bundled here is
renamed anyway, as a precaution: each is either instanced (Roboto Mono) or
subset (the JetBrains Mono Nerd Font Mono and Adwaita Mono cuts), which OFL
treats as a Modified Version, and renaming avoids any appearance that a
modified, subsetted build is the certified upstream font. Each file's name
table (IDs 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 16, 17, and 21 where present) was rewritten to a
family dux owns instead: the two Roboto Mono weights become "Dux Mono"
(Regular / Bold), the JetBrains subset becomes "Dux Mono Symbols", and the
Adwaita subset becomes "Dux Mono Fill". The copyright, author and license
records (IDs 0, 8, 9, 13, 14) are preserved untouched in every file.

Reproduction, from the exact upstream files named above:

  # Roboto Mono: instance the variable font at wght=400 and wght=700,
  # then rename the family in each instanced TTF's name table (fontTools
  # ttLib, IDs 1/2/3/4/6/16/17 -> "Dux Mono" / "Regular" or "Bold"),
  # then compress each to woff2 (fontTools ttLib, flavor="woff2").
  fonttools varLib.instancer RobotoMono[wght].ttf wght=400 -o RobotoMono-Regular.ttf
  fonttools varLib.instancer RobotoMono[wght].ttf wght=700 -o RobotoMono-Bold.ttf
  # (rename + woff2 compress as described above)

  # JetBrains Mono Nerd Font Mono: subset to the structural unicode ranges,
  # rename the family to "Dux Mono Symbols", then compress to woff2.
  pyftsubset JetBrainsMonoNerdFontMono-Regular.ttf \
    --unicodes="U+2190-21FF,U+2300-23FF,U+2500-25FF,U+2600-27BF,U+2800-28FF,U+E0A0-E0D7" \
    --output-file=symbols-subset.ttf \
    --glyph-names --symbol-cmap --legacy-cmap --notdef-glyph --notdef-outline \
    --recommended-glyphs --name-IDs='*' --name-legacy --name-languages='*'
  # (rename + woff2 compress as described above)

  # Adwaita Mono: the code point list is DERIVED rather than hand-written,
  # so unlike the two recipes above this one is not self-contained: it
  # needs the two shipped faces to compute against, and the four exclusion
  # filters cannot be expressed to pyftsubset. Take every code point in
  # U+2000-2BFF, U+2E00-2E7F and U+1F000-1FBFF that AdwaitaMono-Regular.ttf
  # maps, subtract everything already in dux-mono-regular.woff2's and
  # dux-mono-symbols.woff2's cmaps, then drop advance != 600,
  # Emoji_Presentation=Yes, and East_Asian_Width W or F. That yields 2267
  # code points. pyftsubset retains a small closure beyond what it is
  # asked for (it kept U+2215, which the text face already has), so the
  # cmap is pruned back to exactly the requested list afterwards, in the
  # same pass that rewrites the name table.
  pyftsubset AdwaitaMono-Regular.ttf \
    --unicodes-file=derived-codepoints.txt \
    --output-file=fill-subset.ttf \
    --no-hinting --desubroutinize --layout-features= \
    --drop-tables+=GSUB,GPOS,GDEF \
    --glyph-names --symbol-cmap --legacy-cmap --notdef-glyph --notdef-outline \
    --recommended-glyphs --name-IDs='*' --name-legacy --name-languages='*'
  # (prune the cmap to the requested list, rename, compress as above)

